(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-quiz"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which mimic of deep vein thrombosis presents with leg pain, erythema, fever and chills?","choices":["Lymphedema","Cellulitis","Ruptured popliteal cyst","Calf muscle tear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis causes leg pain with erythema and may be accompanied by fever and chills, features that distinguish it from a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which form of immobility counts as an item in the Wells criteria?","choices":["A long-haul airline flight","A car trip of several hours","A desk job with long sitting","Bedridden for more than 3 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Wells item is being bedridden for more than 3 days or having major surgery within 12 weeks. Ordinary travel or sitting is not scored. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which examination findings characterize a healthy hemodialysis fistula?","choices":["Faint thrill with no bruit","Tender cord with overlying warmth","Cool skin and absent pulses","Diffuse thrill and soft bruit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A healthy fistula has a diffuse thrill you can palpate and a soft bruit you can hear, both with systolic and diastolic components, and it collapses with arm elevation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which surgery for chronic venous insufficiency is occasionally successful yet has a high rate of postoperative deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Valvuloplasty","Venous bypass","Vein stripping","Injection sclerotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Valvuloplasty is occasionally successful but the incidence of postoperative deep vein thrombosis is high; the surgical goal is to occlude or remove affected vessels and restore proper flow. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In the high likelihood Wells category, what does a negative D-dimer change?","choices":["It calls for venography","It allows anticoagulation to wait","Nothing; ultrasound is still done","It rules out the clot"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the high Wells category the D-dimer is skipped or, if taken and negative, still followed by ultrasound; the rule-out role applies only at low likelihood. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How is a congenital arteriovenous fistula usually managed?","choices":["Conservatively, with elastic support hose","Excision of its single communication","Lifelong anticoagulation","Thrombolysis with a filter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Congenital fistulas are difficult to treat because the communications can be numerous, so they are treated conservatively with elastic support hose. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which drug class commonly causes bilateral leg swelling without signs of inflammation?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Calcium channel blockers","Low molecular weight heparins","Direct factor Xa inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine commonly cause drug-induced edema, which is often bilateral and usually lacks any sign of inflammation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Without treatment, what is the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism when the embolism is symptomatic and massive?","choices":["Under 1%","About 3%","About 30%","About 9%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Untreated, symptomatic massive pulmonary embolism carries about 9% cumulative fatality, versus 3% for nonmassive embolism and under 1% for deep vein thrombosis alone. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Prior deep vein thrombosis is one of the most common causes of which chronic condition?","choices":["Superficial thrombophlebitis","Arteriovenous fistula","Peripheral artery disease","Chronic venous insufficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prior deep vein thrombosis, through valve scarring and obstruction, is one of the most common causes of chronic venous insufficiency, also called post-thrombotic syndrome. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the gold standard test to confirm superficial thrombophlebitis?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer measurement","Contrast ascending venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis is based on history and examination and is confirmed by venous duplex ultrasound, the gold standard, which shows a noncompressible superficial vein with clot and a thickened wall. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which are the most commonly affected proximal veins in lower extremity deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Saphenous and perforator","Femoral and popliteal","Peroneal and tibial","Iliac and caval"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The femoral and popliteal veins in the thigh are the proximal veins most often involved; the peroneal veins of the calf are the most common distal site. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which finding is most sensitive for a lower extremity deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Leg warmth","A positive Homan sign","Leg swelling","Leg pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leg swelling is 97% sensitive, ahead of leg pain (86%) and warmth (72%); its high sensitivity makes it the finding most often present. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Thrombolysis","Inferior vena cava filter","Thrombectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is the mainstay of treatment; some patients also need thrombolysis or lifelong anticoagulation, depending on the situation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which patients need no D-dimer and go straight to ultrasound under the Wells criteria?","choices":["Those with a score of 3 or more","Those in the low likelihood group","Those with a score of 1 to 2","Those with an alternative diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a Wells score of 3 or more, deep vein thrombosis is likely, so a D-dimer is not needed first and venous duplex ultrasound is ordered directly. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Erythema extending well beyond the margin of an inflamed vein raises suspicion of which condition?","choices":["Lipodermatosclerosis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial phlebitis","Stasis dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When erythema extends significantly beyond the margin of the vein, septic thrombophlebitis should be suspected; simple thrombophlebitis keeps its redness along the vein's course. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which oral agent may help accelerate healing of venous ulcers?","choices":["Furosemide","Warfarin","Aspirin","Prednisone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin may help accelerate venous ulcer healing, though no oral medication has been proven useful for treating venous disease; flavonoids are used to increase venous tone. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How long is anticoagulation given after a venous thromboembolism provoked by a minor transient risk factor?","choices":["9 to 12 months","An indefinite period","3 to 6 months","Less than 3 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Minor transient risk factors, such as estrogen therapy, pregnancy, minor surgery, prolonged travel or minor leg injury, are treated for 3 to 6 months. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why can a positive D-dimer not confirm deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Many conditions raise it","It is often falsely negative","It detects only arterial clots","It shows the clot location"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive D-dimer means fibrinolysis is occurring somewhere in the body, which many conditions cause, so imaging is needed to show a clot in the leg veins. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Older adults with a lower extremity wound need which measurement alongside the arterial pulse examination?","choices":["Skin punch biopsy","Contrast venography","Ankle-brachial index","D-dimer level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Arterial pulse examination combined with the ankle-brachial index should be performed on any patient with a lower extremity wound and in older patients, to rule out peripheral artery disease. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which chronic complication follows deep vein thrombosis 6 to 24 months later?","choices":["Post-thrombotic syndrome","Lymphedema","Arteriovenous fistula","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis, appearing from 6 to 24 months afterward. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which added factors raise the deep vein thrombosis risk of oral contraceptive pill use?","choices":["Regular exercise","Low body weight","A vegetarian diet","Tobacco use or obesity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The clotting risk of estrogen-containing contraceptives is greater still when the patient smokes tobacco or is obese, adding to the hypercoagulable state the hormone creates. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How long is conservative stocking therapy generally tried before ablation is offered for varicose veins?","choices":["One to two years","Weeks to months","A single visit","Several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trial of conservative therapy with stockings is generally done for weeks to months before venous ablation is offered. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What underlies the wide spectrum of abnormalities in chronic venous disease?","choices":["Chronic arterial hypoperfusion","Lymphatic vessel obstruction","Loss of plasma protein into tissue","Venous hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Elevated venous pressure underlies the whole spectrum, from telangiectasias to varicose veins to chronic venous insufficiency, and triggers venous dilation, then skin changes and skin ulcers. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is first-line management of uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis?","choices":["Inferior vena cava filter","Anticoagulation for three months","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Thrombolysis with alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Typical treatment is warm compresses, compression and elevation of the limb, with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen or diclofenac for pain and inflammation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Intermittent pneumatic compression prevents clots chiefly by countering which component of the Virchow triad?","choices":["Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","All three components"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Compression devices squeeze the legs rhythmically like the calf muscles, keeping blood moving so it does not pool; the device counters the stasis component in patients immobilized after surgery. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Andexanet alfa is the reversal agent for which pair of anticoagulants?","choices":["Fondaparinux and dabigatran","Apixaban and rivaroxaban","Dabigatran and warfarin","Heparin and enoxaparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Andexanet alfa reverses apixaban and rivaroxaban, the direct factor Xa inhibitors, and its use is off-label for edoxaban as well. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Prolonged bedridden inactivity raises superficial thrombophlebitis risk. Which Virchow triad component does immobilization represent?","choices":["Arterial insufficiency","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immobilization, including prolonged sitting and postoperative inactivity, is a cause of venous stasis, one of the three Virchow triad components alongside hypercoagulable state and endothelial trauma. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for chronic venous disease?","choices":["Ligamentous laxity (flat feet)","Male sex","Lower body mass index","Regular calf muscle exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ligamentous laxity, for example flat feet, is a listed risk factor, along with advancing age, female sex, family history, higher body mass index, smoking, prior thrombosis and high estrogen states. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which three components make up the Virchow triad?","choices":["Stasis, hypertension, endothelial trauma","Stasis, infection, vessel spasm","Immobility, anemia, dehydration","Stasis, hypercoagulability, endothelial trauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad groups the risk factors for deep vein thrombosis into venous stasis (altered blood flow), a hypercoagulable state and endothelial (vessel wall) trauma. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which study is the gold standard for diagnosing deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Plasma D-dimer","Magnetic resonance venography","Contrast ascending venography","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Contrast ascending venography is the definitive gold standard, but because it is invasive and needs injected dye it has been replaced in practice by ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
